flux-freezing-93fc970f.conceptual_definition:
The Flux-Freezing phenomenon, also known as magnetic flux quantization, states that the magnetic flux (
        \(\Phi\)
        ) passing through a closed superconducting loop is quantized, meaning it can only exist in discrete integer multiples (
        \(n\)
        ) of a fundamental constant known as the magnetic flux quantum (
        \(\Phi_0\)
        ). This is a direct consequence of the macroscopic quantum nature of superconductivity and the formation of Cooper pairs as charge carriers.
